# KNOWN GAP — `gg fmt` MOVES A COMMENT OUT OF THE CALL IT ANNOTATES.
# MUST EXCLUDE from any bulk `gg fmt` sweep: this fixture's SUBJECT is fmt
# output, and sweeping it bakes the defect in as the canonical form.
#
# When the LAST argument of a call carries an AUTHOR GROUPING PAREN, an
# interior comment after it escapes the call and re-parents to the enclosing
# block. Measured at HEAD (2026-08-17):
#
#     sink(1, (2 + 3)
#         # after the paren'd last arg
#     )
#   becomes
#     sink(1, (2 + 3))
#     # after the paren'd last arg      <- OUTSIDE the call, at block indent
#
# CONTROL (same shape, no author paren) is CORRECT — the comment stays inside:
#     sink(1, 2 + 3
#         # control
#     )
#   becomes
#     sink(
#         1,
#         2 + 3,
#         # control
#     )
# so the author paren is the discriminator.
#
# Mechanism: `parse_paren_expr` (src/parser/expr.rs:1658-1660) returns the
# INNER node and pushes the grouping paren to a sideband, so the element span
# EXCLUDES the author's `)`. `paren_tuple_gate`'s `)` scan
# (src/formatter/mod.rs:1777) then locks onto the AUTHOR's paren rather than
# the call's, truncating `container_end` so the comment falls outside the
# gated window.
#
# ⚠ The damaged output is IDEMPOTENT, so no round-trip or reparse gate can
# see this — only an explicit placement assertion can.
#
# Distinct from the three residual rows filed at TODO.md's fill-emitter
# chokepoint entry: those are "not fill-emitted at all"; here the gate IS
# present and its WINDOW is wrong.
#
# INTENDED: the comment stays inside the call, exactly as the control does.
# Wired test: known_gap_fmt_comment_escapes_call_with_author_paren
# (tests/integration.rs, #[ignore]d).
# TWO COSTUMES, both measured 2026-08-17. The paren-at-the-TAIL costume is the
# one a naive fix MISSES: `author_paren_layers` is keyed by the EXACT span of the
# WRAPPED node, while `parse_infix` spans a binary as `lhs.merge(paren-stripped
# rhs)`, so a lookup on the element span returns 0 for `x + (y)` even though the
# author's `)` still sits before the delimiter. The fix must advance past every
# layer whose span ENDS AT the element's end, not just an exact-span hit.
void sink(int a, int b):
    print(a + b)

int add1(int a):
    return a

void main():
    # COSTUME 1 — the element IS the parenthesised expression
    sink(1, (2 + 3)
        # after the paren'd last arg
    )
    int y = 2
    # COSTUME 2 — the paren sits at the TAIL of a larger element
    print(add1(
        1 + (y)
        # tail of a binary whose rhs is parenthesised
    ))
